Ask the user before handing back a saved password.

Chromium had no DeviceAuthenticator here, so GetDeviceAuthenticator returned
null and every path that should have asked went straight through. Combined
with Login Data being readable offline, that is why the manager was switched
off wholesale.

The prompt is HarmonyOS's own -- whatever the user enrolled at ATL3, face,
fingerprint or the lock-screen credential -- reached through the shell's
UserAuthService over the existing system-service channel. Chromium never draws
a password field of its own.

The sixty-second reuse window lives in the OHOS authenticator rather than in
DeviceAuthenticatorProxy, whose timestamp can be set but never cleared. It has
to be cleared: going to the background and moving between ordinary and
incognito windows end it early, and so does locking the screen, which the
engine watches for itself through the common-event NDK rather than relying on
the shell being alive to report it. The clock is monotonic, so changing the
system time cannot extend it.

Filling is the one path with a setting, ohos.password_fill_requires_auth, on
by default. Showing, copying, editing and exporting always ask. The desktop
answer to "does filling need authentication" goes through
PasswordFeatureManager, which reads a local-state pref written by a Views
promo bubble this platform does not have, so IS_OHOS answers from the shell's
setting directly. With nothing enrolled the answer is no -- there is nothing
to ask with -- and what protects the store in that case is HUKS.

IDS_PASSWORD_MANAGER_FILLING_REAUTH, which names the site in the prompt, was
defined for iOS, ChromeOS, Windows and macOS only. The ChromeOS wording fits
and is already translated, so its condition is widened rather than a new
string added that would read English in every locale.

With nothing enrolled, an explicit request to see a password is refused rather
than granted. Opening stored passwords to whoever is holding an unlocked phone
would be worse than refusing, and refusing is what the product asked for.

ohos-password-manager-off.patch is deleted alongside this. It named the HUKS
key provider and this authenticator as its conditions for coming out, and both
now exist; leaving a disabled copy in the tree would only invite someone to
wonder which one is live.

The source files are registered by ohos-huks-oscrypt.patch rather than here,
because both land together and two patches adding lines to the same part of
chrome/browser/BUILD.gn put each other's hunks out of context.

Generated for Chromium 154.0.8037.51 after chromium-154-harmonyos.patch.

